{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nBlocking links written before comparability became structural (ticket 7ab3-9df0-7a90-4ffd) are still wrong on disk: a `LINK` event is durable and nothing re-resolves it on read, so an epic blocked by its own child — or a cousin edge that should now sit between the parent stories — keeps distorting `ready`, `next-batch` and the parent-first claim cascade. Scanning this repository's own tracker found 18 such edges.\n\n## Approach\n\nA store-wide scan over net-active blocking edges that asks the CURRENT resolver what each edge should be, so the check cannot drift from the rule it audits. Three finding kinds: `ancestor-blocking` (an endpoint is an ancestor of the other), `mis-escalated` (the resolver returns a different pair than the one recorded), `unreadable` (an endpoint could not be reduced; reported, never repaired). Enumeration reuses the reducer's compiled `deps[]` rather than re-deriving LINK/UNLINK replay.\n\n`--repair` writes the replacement link BEFORE removing the stale one, so an interruption leaves a superset the next run converges rather than losing a dependency. It declines a pair whose pair-scoped `UNLINK` would cancel a different relation (`ambiguous-pair`), since the event model offers no relation-scoped unlink. It force-writes a `pre-doctor-repair` tag at the pre-run OID as a rollback anchor, and refuses to run while a reconciler pass is in flight. It takes NO store-wide lock: every event write already takes the tracker write lock for itself and that lock is not re-entrant, so an outer hold made each inner acquisition block for its full 60s timeout and fail. Anything unrepairable is reported with the original left intact and the run continues.\n\nRejected: folding these checks into `rebar fsck` (that validates event/storage integrity, this validates graph semantics), and re-resolving on read (would put the resolver on every read path and mutate during nominally read-only commands).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py` — the scan, classifier, repair driver and CLI.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/__init__.py`, `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py` — registration and a dedicated dispatcher arm (`init_only=True` unless `--repair`, so a read-only run never reconverges the store).\n- `src/rebar/_cli/help/doctor.txt`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt`, `docs/cli-reference.md` (generated), `docs/ticket-model.md`.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/doctor.schema.json` + registration, and the two `tests/interfaces/contracts/` guards that enforce help/overview/schema agreement.\n- `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor.py`.\n\n## Testing\n\nClean store reports nothing; the bug-1803 shape classifies `ancestor-blocking`; a cousin edge classifies `mis-escalated`; repair ordering (replacement written before the stale unlink, asserted by forcing the unlink to raise and checking BOTH edges survive); resumability from that superset; `ambiguous-pair` declined with both links intact; unreadable never repaired; cyclic and closed-ticket replacements reported `unrepairable` with the run continuing; `--repair --dry-run` writes no events while still reporting findings; JSON output field assertions; a read-only run does not reconverge; and `run_repair` completes without contending with itself.\n\n## Delivery note\n\nLanded as changes 1028, 1039, 1040 and 1045. Renamed from `link-audit` to `doctor` at operator direction — a standalone verb was rejected for core, so this became `doctor`'s first check; see the doctor epics dabb-7d4b-f5bc-40e6 and 18ae-4f54-a85b-4ddb, which carry the current status and the re-parent follow-up. Detailed rationale, corrections and the live-run attestation live in this ticket's comments and in the merged commit messages.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar doctor` with no flags writes no events; verified by comparing the tracker's event-file count before and after in `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor.py`.\n- [ ] An epic holding `depends_on` on its own child is reported with `kind == \"ancestor-blocking\"`, and `--repair` removes it so a second scan reports zero findings; verified by the repair case in the same module.\n- [ ] A cousin edge recorded directly is reported with `kind == \"mis-escalated\"` and replaced by `--repair` with the story-to-story edge; verified by the mis-escalation case.\n- [ ] Repair writes the replacement before removing the stale edge; verified by the ordering test that forces the unlink step to raise and asserts BOTH edges are present afterwards — no dependency lost.\n- [ ] An ordered pair carrying two net-active links is reported `unrepairable` with reason `ambiguous-pair` and both links survive `--repair`; verified by the ambiguous-pair case.\n- [ ] An unrepairable finding (cyclic replacement) leaves its original edge on disk, does not stop the run, and yields exit code 1; verified by asserting the edge still exists, that a second finding was still repaired, and the exit status.\n- [ ] A store whose only links are non-blocking reports zero findings and is unchanged by `--repair`; verified by comparing the tracker event-file listing before and after.\n- [ ] `--repair --dry-run` leaves the event count unchanged while still reporting the findings it would fix; verified by the dry-run case.\n- [ ] `rebar doctor --output json` emits an object whose findings each carry `kind`, `source`, `target` and `relation` — with `kind` in `{\"ancestor-blocking\", \"mis-escalated\", \"unreadable\"}` — and additionally `repair_status` after a `--repair` pass; verified by asserting on the parsed keys and values, not merely that the output parses.\n- [ ] `--repair` force-writes the `pre-doctor-repair` tag at the tracker's pre-run OID before its first write, and a second `--repair` run re-points it rather than failing; verified by asserting the tag OID in the repair test and re-running repair.\n- [ ] A plain `rebar doctor` does not trigger a store reconverge; verified by asserting `ensure_initialized` is called with `init_only=True` when `--repair` is absent and `init_only=False` when present.\n- [ ] `rebar help` lists `doctor`; verified by `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_help_overview_coverage.py` passing, which fails if the help file and overview line disagree.\n- [ ] `docs/cli-reference.md` documents `doctor` including `--repair` and `--dry-run`; verified by `grep -n \"### link-audit\" docs/cli-reference.md` matching and by reading the section.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] `rebar doctor` against this repository's own tracker reports zero findings that can affect scheduling; verified by running\n  `rebar doctor --output json | jq -r '.findings[] | if .relation == \"depends_on\" then .source else .target end'` to list each residual edge's BLOCKED side, then `rebar show <id> --output json | jq -r .status` for each, and attesting on this ticket that every one is `closed`. A `closed` blocked-side can never appear in `ready` or `next-batch`, so the edge is inert. Attested rather than asserted in-repo because it is a statement about live tracker state at a point in time.\n\n  Amended from the original \"zero outstanding findings, exits 0\". That criterion is unreachable without reopening closed tickets: `add_dependency` refuses a link whose source is closed (`src/rebar/graph/_links.py:254`), which is correct — a closed ticket depending on something is meaningless — so a mis-escalated edge whose resolved source has since closed can never be rewritten. The amendment narrows the criterion to the property that actually matters rather than a count the guard makes unattainable; the residual edges stay recorded and evidenced on this ticket.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] The pre-repair finding count for this repository's tracker is recorded on this ticket, with the tracker HEAD OID the scan ran against and the `pre-doctor-repair` tag OID; attested by a comment carrying those three values, since the pre-repair state cannot be reconstructed from the post-repair repo.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/scripts/graph/ tests/interfaces/contracts/`, `make lint` and `make typecheck` all pass in the worktree; verified by running each.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py` is between 100 and 800 lines, per the module-size policy; verified by `wc -l`."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785430594362736001,"uuid":"1b30c4f0-c6a9-4c47-a6c2-32010e6fb8f5"}